--- Rosetta Code: Topic variable
--- https://rosettacode.org/wiki/Topic_variable
--- Crow has no topic variable: no $_, it or ans stands for "the current
--- value", in a program or in the REPL (crow with no file shows each line's
--- value, and gives the last one no name). The spec weighed one, `it` for the
--- value flowing through a chain, and chose names instead (Design Note: a
--- chain's steps name the variable). The nearest idioms are shown below, each
--- with how it nests: `then`, which hands a value on unnamed as the next
--- call's first argument; a chain written as lines, where the variable being
--- assigned names the value so far; and a loop's variable, the current item.

fn square[x]
    x * x

log['Crow has no topic variable. The nearest idioms:']
log['']

--- `then`: the value goes into the next call as its first argument, unnamed
log['then hands a value on unnamed, as the next call\'s first argument:']
log['  3 then square = `3 then square`']
log['  3 then sqrt = `3 then sqrt`']
log['  3 then square then sqrt = `3 then square then sqrt`']
log['']

--- a chain as lines: at each step the variable being assigned is the value
--- so far, so the name changes what it holds from line to line
t = 3
    then t * t
    then t + sqrt[t]
log['A chain written as lines names the value so far by the variable it assigns:']
log['  t = 3, then t * t (t is 3 there), then t + sqrt[t] (t is 9 there): t = `t`']
--- a `then` nested inside a step hands that step's value on in its turn
u = 3
    then u + (u then sqrt)
log['  u = 3, then u + (u then sqrt): u = `u`']
log['']

--- a loop's variable: the current item, named
log['A loop\'s variable is the current item:']
for x in [3, 4]
    log['  x = `x`: its square `x * x`, its square root `sqrt[x]`']
roots = for x in [1, 4, 9]
    sqrt[x]
log['  a collecting loop, the item named once: roots of 1, 4, 9 = `roots`']
log['']

--- nesting: a loop's variable hides one of the same name in the loop around
--- it, for the inner loop's body only
log['Nested loops: the inner x hides the outer x, which is back after the inner loop:']
for x in [1, 2]
    before = x
    inner = for x in [10, 20]
        x
    log['  outer x = `before`; the inner loop\'s x took `inner`; after it, x = `x`']
log['']

--- scope: nothing is ambient. A function sees only its parameters, so the
--- current item reaches it only when it is handed over
log['A function sees only its parameters: the current item reaches it as an argument,']
squares = for x in [1, 2, 3]
    square[x]
log['  as in square[x] for x in 1, 2, 3 = `squares`']
